m-boot bootable-media menu

PURPOSE
    The external ESP-resident loader offers "Bootable media" in its main
    menu, with Recovery and backup, Live systems and installers, Hardware
    diagnostics, and Network boot categories. It discovers the data
    partition by the MBOOTDATA label or /m/boot/iso, and presents files
    without changing ext4 or requiring a rebuild for payload copies.

DATA ROOT (PATHS RETAINED, EXT4 DEFAULT 2026-10-31)
    ext4: /m/boot/iso/ (flat ISO list)
           /m/boot/efi/memtest/ (unpacked memtest86+ binaries)
           /m/boot/efi/netboot/ (netboot.xyz EFI, PXE and Linux images)
    ESP:   /m/boot/loader/ (all GRUB scripts, menus, modules and themes)

    Current product images provision ext4; the GRUB exFAT reader remains
    for older USB payloads during migration, without fixing Ubuntu's
    exFAT early-userspace limitation.

MENU ENTRY SUPPORT LEVELS
    Existing user-maintained scripts are retained. Recipes under
    loader/menu/iso-boot/ from the historical Multiboot USB project
    (https://github.com/aguslr/multibootusb, mbusb.d/) are reused only
    for selected matching images (Clonezilla, GParted, Grml, Ubuntu).
    Their compatibility with the user's 2026 ISO revisions has NOT
    been established by firmware boot tests.

    Other visible payload entries say "boot recipe pending" and only
    identify the file; they do not pretend to boot it. EFI netboot.xyz
    choices on matching UEFI architecture are marked experimental because
    UEFI/GRUB chainloading from ext4 is not yet validated. BIOS PXE
    images and Memtest86+ binaries are not guessed to be EFI executables.

VERIFICATION
    Build and script syntax checks are not equivalent to a firmware boot.
    Test actual menu navigation on BIOS x86_64, UEFI x86_64 and UEFI ARM64.
    Validate each selected ISO family through the running target system
    or installer, not just the first GRUB menu. Some kernels/initramfs
    may lack the needed ext4/ISO loop access.

    Editing ESP loader scripts after media provisioning requires updating
    the ESP loader; the original USB need not be destructively reflashed.
    The Git-tracked public disk image does not update an already-flashed
    USB device by itself.

    No historical Windows/NaVi recipes have been re-enabled.
